.TH CHECK-PROJECT-BUSY 1 "2026-08-18" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
check-project-busy \- is a scheduler-dispatched job running against a project right now?
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B check-project-busy
.I project
.SH DESCRIPTION
Probes a project's dispatch locks and prints
.B BUSY
or
.BR free .
Run it before writing DIRECTLY into another project's files; on
.B BUSY
defer the write and say what you deferred.
.PP
It is a live
.BR "kill -0" (2)
probe against the recorded pid, not a flag file, so a stale lock left by a
killed job reads as free rather than blocking the estate forever.
.PP
It does NOT gate FRONT-DOOR writes \(em
.BR "scheduler -i" ,
.BR notify-senechal (1)
\(em which carry their own regulator. Using it there is redundant, and
treating a front door as if it needed this check is how a write that was
never at risk gets deferred.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Answered, whether BUSY or free.
.TP
.B 2
No such project, or no schedule entry for the name given. It refuses to
answer "free" for a name it cannot check.
.SH NOTES
Reads the scheduler's own
.I schedule/<project>.conf
under
.IR $INSTALLE_PROJECTS ,
so a host with no scheduler checkout gets a refusal rather than a guess.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR notify-senechal (1),
.BR discipline (1)
